# Background jobs

`howdy_jobs` keeps work to be done later in your app's database, and does
it outside the request. It gives you:

- jobs queued inside your own transactions, so a job exists only if the
  change that needs it was committed
- retries with a growing delay, unique jobs, delayed jobs and priorities
- [schedules](/docs/jobs/schedules) in cron's notation, queued once per
  time however many nodes run them
- [jobs spread across nodes](/docs/jobs/production), with a job that a
  stopped node left half done run again elsewhere
- a page in the [admin area](/docs/admin/overview), a command line for
  servers, and a span for every job in your [traces](/docs/telemetry/overview)

It works on PostgreSQL and SQLite, through the same Repo as the rest of
your app.

## Install

Add the package to your project, next to `howdy_database`:

```sh
gleam add howdy_jobs@1
```

Its tables are created by a migration package, like any other:

```gleam
import howdy/jobs
import howdy/migration

migration.run(db, [auth.schema(), jobs.schema(), my_app.schema()])
```

## Defining a job

A job is defined once, as a function, with a name and how its arguments
are stored:

```gleam
import gleam/dynamic/decode
import gleam/json
import howdy/jobs

pub fn welcome_email() -> jobs.Job(String) {
  jobs.define("welcome_email", encode: json.string, decoder: decode.string)
  |> jobs.on_queue("mail")
  |> jobs.max_attempts(5)
}
```

The definition is used where the job is queued and where it is done, so
it needs nothing but itself: no database, no mailer. The name is how a
stored job finds the code that does it, so do not change it once jobs have
been queued under it. Names and queue names are letters, digits, `_`, `-`,
`.` and `:`.

Arguments are stored as JSON. Keep them small, such as IDs rather than
whole records: the job reads what it needs when it runs, and so sees the
data as it is then. A job queued before a deploy runs after it with the
new decoder, so make decoders accept what older code stored.

A job that needs no arguments is `jobs.without_args("nightly_cleanup")`.

The definition also says, for every job of its kind:

- `jobs.on_queue(job, queue)` which queue it goes to, `default` unless
  you say
- `jobs.priority(job, n)` from 0 to 9: due jobs of a queue go 0 first
- `jobs.max_attempts(job, n)` how many times it is tried in all, 20 by
  default
- `jobs.timeout(job, milliseconds)` how long one attempt may take, five
  minutes by default
- `jobs.backoff(job, fn(attempt) { milliseconds })` how long to wait
  before trying again after attempt number `attempt` failed. The default
  doubles from 15 seconds up to six hours.

## Queueing a job

`jobs.new` takes the definition and the arguments, and `jobs.insert` adds
the job to the database:

```gleam
use conn <- database.transaction(db)
use user <- result.try(users.insert(conn, form))
use _ <- result.try(
  jobs.new(welcome_email(), user.id) |> jobs.insert(into: conn),
)
Ok(user)
```

Give `insert` the connection of the transaction that makes the job
necessary. If the transaction rolls back, the job was never queued; if it
commits, the job is there, however the request ends. There is no window
in which the user exists but their email was never queued, or the email
goes out for a user who was never saved.

Runners on the same node are told about the job when the transaction
commits, and on PostgreSQL every other node hears about it too, so a job
due now starts at once.

### When

- `jobs.after(insert, milliseconds)` waits that long first.
- `jobs.at(insert, unix_milliseconds)` waits until then.

A runner takes the job once it is due. A job that is due waits only for a
free slot in its queue.

### Only once

`jobs.unique(insert, key)` queues the job only if no unfinished job has the
same key:

```gleam
jobs.new(reindex(), document.id)
|> jobs.unique("reindex:" <> int.to_string(document.id))
|> jobs.insert(into: conn)
```

`insert` returns `jobs.Inserted(id)` for a new job and `jobs.Duplicate(id)`
with the waiting or running job's id otherwise. Once that job has finished,
the key is free again. Combined with `after`, it gathers a burst into one
job: a digest that waits a minute is queued once however many notes are
written in that minute.

`jobs.unique_while_waiting(insert, key)` is the same, except that a job
that has started no longer holds the key. Use it where work that arrives
while the job runs must not be lost: a change made while a search index is
being rebuilt queues another rebuild, rather than finding the running one
and being dropped. If a job like this fails while another with its key is
waiting, it is cancelled instead of retried, since the waiting one will do
the work; that one is brought forward to when the retry would have run, if
it was due later.

`jobs.insert_all(inserts, into: conn)` queues several jobs in order.

## Doing jobs

A runner is what does jobs. It has the code for each kind and the queues
to take them from:

```gleam
pub fn runner(db: Repo, mailer: mail.Mailer) -> jobs.Runner {
  jobs.runner(db)
  |> jobs.queue("default", concurrency: 10)
  |> jobs.queue("mail", concurrency: 5)
  |> jobs.handle(welcome_email(), fn(user_id, _attempt) {
    send_welcome(db, mailer, user_id)
  })
}
```

`concurrency` is how many of the queue's jobs run at once on each node. A
queue a handled job uses is taken from anyway, ten at a time, unless it is
given.

Run it under your app's supervisor, before the server, so a stop lets
running jobs finish after the server has stopped taking requests:

```gleam
supervisor.new(supervisor.OneForOne)
|> supervisor.add(jobs.supervised(runner(db, mailer)))
|> supervisor.add(howdy.supervised(app))
|> howdy.start_application(name: "my_app")
```

See [Supervision and shutdown](/docs/guides/supervision).

### What a job says

A handler gets the job's arguments and the attempt being made, and says
what came of it:

- `jobs.Done`: it is finished.
- `jobs.Retry(reason)`: it may work later, such as when a service it calls
  is down. It is tried again after the job's backoff, unless that was its
  last attempt, and then it fails.
- `jobs.RetryIn(milliseconds, reason)`: the same, after this long.
- `jobs.Snooze(milliseconds)`: not now, such as when a rate limit says to
  wait. It is tried again after this long, and it does not count as an
  attempt.
- `jobs.Cancel(reason)`: it is no longer needed, such as when the user it
  was for has gone. It is not tried again.
- `jobs.GiveUp(reason)`: it never will work. It fails without being tried
  again.

A handler that crashes, or runs past its timeout, made an attempt that
failed: it is tried again like `Retry`. Each failure is kept with the job,
the reason and when, and the admin and the command line show them.

The `Attempt` says which attempt this is (`attempt.number`, from 1), how
many it gets, when the job was queued and the failures so far.
`jobs.is_last(attempt)` is true on the last one, for a handler that wants
to tell someone before it gives up.

```gleam
fn send_welcome(db, mailer, user_id) -> jobs.Outcome {
  case users.get(db, user_id) {
    Error(service.NotFound(_)) -> jobs.Cancel("the user is gone")
    Error(error) -> jobs.Retry(service.message(error))
    Ok(user) ->
      case mail.send(mailer, welcome(user)) {
        Ok(_) -> jobs.Done
        Error(error) ->
          case mail.retryable(error) {
            True -> jobs.Retry(mail.error_to_string(error))
            False -> jobs.GiveUp(mail.error_to_string(error))
          }
      }
  }
}
```

### Done at least once

A job is done at least once, not exactly once. While a node runs a job it
keeps renewing its claim on it; if the node stops without finishing, such
as when its machine is lost, the claim runs out and another node runs the
job again. A handler that sent an email and then crashed before saying so
sends it again. Write handlers so that doing a job twice is harmless, such
as by checking first whether the work has been done. See
[Running jobs](/docs/jobs/production).

## Queued email

[Howdy Mail](/docs/mail/overview) sends while the caller waits.
`mail.message_to_json` and `mail.message_decoder` let a whole message be a
job's arguments, so it can be sent from a job instead:

```gleam
pub fn send_email() -> jobs.Job(mail.Message) {
  jobs.define("send_email",
    encode: mail.message_to_json,
    decoder: mail.message_decoder(),
  )
  |> jobs.on_queue("mail")
}

// Queued with the change it is about.
jobs.new(send_email(), receipt(order)) |> jobs.insert(into: conn)

// Done by the runner.
jobs.handle(runner, send_email(), fn(message, _) {
  case mail.send(mailer, message) {
    Ok(_) -> jobs.Done
    Error(error) ->
      case mail.retryable(error) {
        True -> jobs.Retry(mail.error_to_string(error))
        False -> jobs.GiveUp(mail.error_to_string(error))
      }
  }
})
```

Attachments are stored too, a third larger than the files. For big files,
store them elsewhere, pass their keys and attach them in the handler.

## Testing

`jobs.drain(runner)` does every job that is due, in the test's own
process, one after another, until none are left, including jobs those
jobs queue. No runner need be started, and the work is done when it
returns:

```gleam
pub fn signing_up_sends_a_welcome_test() {
  let db = test_database()
  let box = outbox.start()
  let assert Ok(_) = signup(db, "ada@example.com")
  let assert Ok(drained) = jobs.drain(runner(db, mailer(box)))
  assert drained.completed == 1
  let assert Ok(_) = outbox.latest_to(box, "ada@example.com")
}
```

`Drained` counts what was completed, retried, snoozed, cancelled and
failed. A job retried later is not waited for: check it with `jobs.get`
or `jobs.list`.
